Date:   Fri Jul 31 21:13:58 2026 +0300

    on-device-privacy: convert "there is no server either" to a check (#99)

    RULES.md's "the app listens on no socket" was always-testable but only ever
    enforced as prose. Three prior sweeps read it as already covered by
    no-network-client, on the grounds that an NWListener needs `import Network`,
    which that check bans. True, but only for the Network framework — and a
    listener does not have to come through it. socket(2) is reachable from plain
    `import Foundation` (Darwin is re-exported); NSXPCListener, CFSocket* and
    NSSocketPort are Foundation types; an embedded HTTP server (Vapor, SwiftNIO,
    Swifter, GCDWebServer) arrives as an import. None of those trip
    no-network-client — verified end-to-end: a file whose only content is
    `let fd = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0)` passes check_the_world today.

    no-listener is the same absolute ban-list shape as no-network-client, over
    mac/Sources/**.swift. A bare `listen(` is deliberately NOT banned: this is an
    app whose entire domain vocabulary is listening (requestListening,
    finishListening, `private var listening`), so a mic-listening method named
    listen() is a false alarm waiting to happen — and it costs nothing to drop,
    since a BSD socket is created before it is listened on, so `socket(` catches
    the same code one line earlier. A fixture pins that vocabulary.

    The prose paragraph is deleted whole per the deletion test: the check now
    carries the rule (the `why` carries "a decision about exposing the laugh log,
    not a feature to slip in"; the `fix` carries the owner sign-off, loopback-only
    default and auth/CSRF story), and the module header carries the rationale —
    that a web dashboard once existed in the Python reference and was removed on
    purpose. Nothing in the paragraph was left uncarried.

    Fixtures: 14/14 (3 new — every listener shape fires, the mic's listening
    vocabulary stays quiet, the real sources stay quiet). Additionally seen to
    fire against the real AppDelegate.swift under a realistic regression
    (an NWListener property and a raw metrics socket), and end-to-end through
    check_the_world.mjs on a temporary violating file.

    Refs #95.


